  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This guide is for the version of the Water Genasi published in Monsters of\n\tthe Multiverse. For the classic version of the Genasi, see our\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Genasi Handbook",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/races/genasi/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Water genasi are descendants of marids, genies from the elemental plane of\n\twater. This heritage manifests in their traits as spells that manipulate\n\twater, but also as acid resistance and Acid Splash for some reason. I have no\n\tidea. Usually you get cold resistance with water-associated stuff, but what do\n\tI know?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Water Genasi changes surprisingly little when it was reprinted in\n\tMordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. They added a 2nd-level innate\n\tspell, traded Shape Water for Acid Splash (possibly because WotC didn’t want\n\tto reprint Shape Water or because they read about all the crazy stuff you can\n\tdo with it), and moved to the new standard for ability score increases.\n\tNothing really big here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "But the Water Genasi didn’t get any better with these changes. Water Walk is only useful if you’re in a place where water is hazardous (rare in most games) or if you’re in an aquatic campaign. Acid Splash is a decent attack cantrip, but not so good that you won’t be tempted to take others. Overall, the spellcasting just isn’t particularly impressive."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
